          "session_id": "full-g-md-009",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My TSH is a little high but my free T4 is still in the normal range. What does that mean, what does guidance describe about treating it, and how often should it be rechecked?",
          "answer": "If you develop severe tiredness with confusion, marked drowsiness, a very low body temperature, or chest pain or a racing heartbeat, seek urgent medical care.\n\nA slightly high TSH with a normal free T4 is called subclinical hypothyroidism. TSH is a signal from the pituitary gland that tells the thyroid to make hormone, and it tends to rise first when thyroid hormone runs low. Guidance describes this as a pattern on a lab result rather than a diagnosis. A single slightly high TSH does not by itself confirm thyroid disease, because the number can shift with timing, recent illness, and some medicines. Thyroid peroxidase antibody tests can show whether an autoimmune thyroid condition is the cause and help estimate how likely it is to progress (guideline-hypothyroidism-testing-and-monitoring; \"Hypothyroidism: which test comes first\").\n\nOn rechecking, the guidance says an abnormal result should be repeated after several weeks before any long-term treatment starts, and it warns that treating a single abnormal value is a common error. The 6 to 8 week recheck it describes applies to people already on levothyroxine after a start or dose change, not to untreated people, so it does not tell you how often you should be retested (guideline-hypothyroidism-testing-and-monitoring; condition-hypothyroidism).\n\nI could not find what guidance recommends about treating subclinical hypothyroidism, at what TSH level treatment is considered, where authorities disagree, or how strong the trial evidence is. The thyroid guidance refers to a disagreement section and a separate note on this question, but those were not retrieved, so I have not filled these gaps from memory.\n\nQuestions to take to your clinician:\n- Should my TSH be repeated, and when?\n- Would a thyroid antibody test help, and what would the result change?\n- Given my level and health history, what would make treatment worth discussing?\n- What symptoms should prompt an earlier review?\n- Do any of my current medicines or supplements affect thyroid tests?\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
